From: =?UTF-8?q?=D0=9A=D0=B8=D1=80=D0=B8=D0=BB=D0=BE=20=D0=A8=D0=B8=D0=BF=D0=B0=D1=87=D0=BE=D0=B2?= <kshypachov@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 12 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] drivers: ethernet: w5500: block IPv4 multicast in MACRAW mode

The W5500 RX thread is created with K_PRIO_COOP(2) and, inside its
`while (int_pin)` loop, performs synchronous polled SPI without ever
yielding. Any sustained frame rate on the segment therefore starves the
rest of the system rather than merely loading it.

This was reached on real hardware: an unrelated 239.255.x.x stream on the
LAN delivered roughly 271 frames/s to the driver, and logging and the
shell stopped responding for as long as it ran. The frames were discarded
by the IP stack — none of them were for this device — so the entire cost
was paid before anything could decide they were uninteresting.

Socket 0 mode already sets MACRAW plus the MAC filter. Setting MMB
(Sn_MR bit 5) in addition makes the chip drop IPv4 multicast before it
reaches the host, which removes the load at its source.

MIP6B (bit 4) is deliberately left clear. Both bits were characterised on
this hardware: MMB drops only IPv4 multicast, MIP6B drops only IPv6
multicast. Blocking IPv6 multicast breaks Neighbor Discovery and SLAAC,
and Matter runs on IPv6 multicast, so it cannot be used on this product.

The starvation itself is upstream issue #115626, where the consensus is
that these threads should not be cooperative. This patch only removes the
traffic that triggered it here; it is not a fix for that issue.

Signed-off-by: Кирило Шипачов <kshypachov@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500.c b/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500.c
index a7c588bbeb6..3f16a9b0a23 100644
--- a/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500.c
+++ b/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500.c
@@ -419,8 +419,15 @@ static void w5500_iface_init(struct net_if *iface)
 	const struct device *dev = net_if_get_device(iface);
 	struct w5500_runtime *ctx = dev->data;
 
-	/* configure Socket 0 with MACRAW mode and MAC filtering enabled */
-	uint8_t mode = S0_MR_MACRAW | BIT(W5500_S0_MR_MF);
+	/*
+	 * Socket 0 in MACRAW with MAC filtering. MMB additionally drops IPv4
+	 * multicast in hardware: an unrelated 239.255.x stream on the segment
+	 * was delivering ~271 frames/s to this driver, and since the RX thread
+	 * is cooperative it never yielded, starving logging, the shell and the
+	 * rest of the system. IPv6 multicast is left enabled (MIP6B, bit 4,
+	 * stays clear) because Matter depends on it.
+	 */
+	uint8_t mode = S0_MR_MACRAW | BIT(W5500_S0_MR_MF) | BIT(W5500_S0_MR_MMB);
 
 	w5500_spi_write(dev, W5500_S0_MR, &mode, 1);
 
diff --git a/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500_priv.h b/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500_priv.h
index d1070e62d49..a86cd6a5bfe 100644
--- a/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500_priv.h
+++ b/drivers/ethernet/eth_w5500_priv.h
@@ -68,6 +68,8 @@
 #define W5500_S0_IMR		(W5500_S0_REGS + W5500_Sn_IMR)
 
 #define W5500_S0_MR_MF		7 /* MAC Filter for W5500 */
+#define W5500_S0_MR_MMB		5 /* IPv4 multicast blocking in MACRAW mode */
+#define W5500_S0_MR_MIP6B	4 /* IPv6 packet blocking in MACRAW mode */
 #define W5500_Sn_REGS_LEN	0x0040
 #define W5500_SIMR		0x0018 /* Socket Interrupt Mask Register */
 #define IR_S0			0x01
